<p>
  <.link navigate="/reports">Reports</.link>
  in the sidebar holds every standard report, grouped the way you think about your business.
  They read live data, so they're always up to date. You only see the reports for the modules
  your workspace has.
</p>

<h2>Finding a report</h2>
<p>
  Type in the search box at the top of the Reports page (try <em>ageing</em>, <em>VAT</em>
  or <em>ledger</em>), or press <strong>⌘K</strong>
  (<strong>Ctrl+K</strong>) anywhere and type the report's name, then press Enter.
</p>

<h2>Choosing the period</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Period reports</strong>
    (profit and loss, sales, VAT…) have a <em>Report period</em>: this month, last quarter,
    year to date, last year… or pick your own <em>From</em>
    and <em>To</em>
    dates.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>As-of reports</strong>
    (balance sheet, ageing, balances) show the position on one date. Ageing on a past date only
    counts payments made up to that date.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Lists</strong> (contacts, products, open orders) show things as they are today.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  With branches switched on, pick a branch to see only its figures. Click any underlined name
  to open the invoice, bill, account or contact behind it.
</p>

<h2>What's in each group</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Group</th><th>Reports</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Business overview</td>
      <td>
        Balance sheet (with comparison, detail and summary), profit and loss (by month, quarter,
        customer, tag, as % of income, compared with last year or the year to date), cash flows,
        changes in equity, business snapshot, project profitability and the audit log.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Who owes you</td>
      <td>
        Receivables ageing, collections, customer balances, invoice lists, open invoices,
        statements, payment terms and goods delivered but not yet invoiced.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Sales and customers</td>
      <td>
        Sales by customer, product and customer type, income by customer, deposits, estimates
        (quotes), payment methods, contact and phone lists, and transaction lists.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>What you owe</td>
      <td>
        Payables ageing, unpaid bills, supplier balances, bill payments, cheques, purchases by
        product and supplier, and supplier contact lists.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Inventory and orders</td>
      <td>
        Inventory valuation, stock status (on hand, on order, committed), a physical count sheet,
        and open purchase and sales orders.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Sales tax</td>
      <td>VAT detail, summary and exceptions, tax liability and transactions by tax code.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Employees</td>
      <td>
        Employee contact list and the <.link navigate="/help/time-tracking">time</.link> reports.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>For my accountant</td>
      <td>
        Account list, general ledger, journal, trial balance (and adjusted), transaction lists and
        a check for invalid journal entries.
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>A few things worth knowing</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Sales</strong> are invoice amounts before tax, from issued and paid invoices.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Cost of sales and margins</strong>
    use each product's current average cost; services have no cost.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Projects</strong>
    are deals: link an invoice to a deal to see it in project profitability.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Customer type</strong>
    is the company's industry; <strong>tag groups</strong>
    are the tags on contacts.
  </li>
  <li>
    The <strong>adjusted trial balance</strong> treats manual journal entries as adjustments.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Print and export</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Print / PDF</strong>
  prints the report without the menus. <strong>Export CSV</strong>
  downloads it for Excel or Google Sheets, with the same period and branch.
</p>
